Since this episode is a continuation, I thought everyone might appreciate a little update on my legs.

27
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:04.410
Guess what?

28
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:08.790
Oh, I thought you were going to update us on where we were at in the episode, but no.

29
00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:09.970
Oh, I will.

30
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:10.430
Oh, yeah.

31
00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:11.210
We'll get to that.

32
00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:15.510
But first, don't you want to know about my legs, if they're shaved or not?

33
00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:18.530
Did I get a razor in the mail like you suggested?

34
00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:20.130
Can I get a touch test?

35
00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.610
Well, I mean, you can see it.

36
00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:23.550
Let's see it.

37
00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:25.390
Hey!

38
00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:28.050
Look at them shiny gaMs.

39
00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:28.710
Yep.

40
00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.430
Just in time for fall weather.

41
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:31.090
Oh, yeah.

42
00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:32.370
Just in time for short skirts.

43
00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:34.330
Gary being sarcastic.

44
00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:35.670
I am.

45
00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:41.990
Second update, I think we're going to need to make sure that Cassie is still working on the clacky balls.

46
00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:42.610
Oh, yes.

47
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.650
The clacky balls that a toddler destroyed.

48
00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:48.930
The setup she has in here with the lamp, and it's the most focused I've ever seen her.

49
00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:55.970
Well, Cassie, if you untangle the balls, you need to break in immediately to the podcaSt.

50
00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:56.670
Don't wait.

51
00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.090
We want to know right away.

52
00:01:58.310 --> 00:01:58.990
It's a special announcement.

53
00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.610
Now that we got that out of the way, here's your summary.

54
00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:07.110
Remember Michael forced Pam to redo the Christmas party for Holly's return?

55
00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:08.990
Yeah, he wanted a classy Christmas.

56
00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.830
But he learned that Holly is still dating AJ.

57
00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:12.350
Mm-hmm.

58
00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:13.470
They live together.

59
00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:14.010
Yeah.

60
00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.550
Also, Jim and Dwight got in an epic snowball fight.

61
00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:18.850
Well, that's not going to quit.

62
00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:24.530
Daryl brought his daughter Jada to the Christmas party, and he's struggling to keep her entertained.

63
00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:29.750
In this episode, Angela's boyfriend, the senator, is going to show up at the party.

64
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:30.910
Yes, he is.

65
00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:32.010
She's very proud.

66
00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:34.350
She's so smug about it.

67
00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:36.910
Except Oscar's pretty sure he's gay.

68
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:37.710
Yeah.

69
00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:42.210
And then AJ is going to arrive to surprise Holly.

70
00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:43.970
That's what's going on.

71
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.150
And you thought there wouldn't be any drama at the Christmas party.

72
00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:48.510
I know.

73
00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.410
Well, there wouldn't have been any drama at the first Christmas party.

74
00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:51.970
That's true.

75
00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:55.390
So, fast fact number one, we had a fan mail flurry.

76
00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:00.770
It was all about that amazing comic book that Pam makes for Jim.

77
00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.470
You know the one, The Adventures of Jimmy Halpert.

78
00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:05.210
Yeah.

79
00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:06.630
He's the bear man.

80
00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:07.830
The bear man.

81
00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:14.870
In last week's episode, she showed it to Ryan, who didn't have a lot of nice things to say about it.

82
00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:15.330
No.

83
00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:17.670
This episode, she shows it to Phyllis.

84
00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.170
I titled that scene Phyllis Snark.

85
00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:21.150
We'll get to it.

86
00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:22.050
Yeah.

87
00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:24.970
But then she finally gives it to Jim.

88
00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:25.430
Yeah.

89
00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:29.550
The fan mail flurry was all about who created this comic book.

90
00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:31.390
Some people wanted to know, did I draw it?

91
00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.390
They had heard that I did used to do comic book art.

92
00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:35.830
You did comic book art.

93
00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:37.450
I have a little doodle you gave me.

94
00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:38.790
I saved all those years ago.

95
00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:39.870
You drew it on the set.

96
00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:40.710
You're very good.

97
00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:45.090
I got really into autobiographical comics for a period of time.

98
00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:46.910
Joe Matt was one of my favorites.

99
00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:52.050
And then I started kind of drawing out basically almost like diary entries.

100
00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:53.930
So I had a little myself.

101
00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.470
I had a little you.

102
00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:56.330
Yeah.

103
00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:57.410
I had whoever.

104
00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:58.070
Yeah.

105
00:03:58.290 --> 00:03:58.450
Yeah.

106
00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:02.030
For this comic book, I will say I did not draw this.

107
00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:02.990
I'm not this good.

108
00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.410
You know, you see glimpses of it in the episode.

109
00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:06.530
It's very, very good.

110
00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:12.590
To find someone to do this comic book, Phil Shea reached out to a comic book shop called

111
00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:18.810
DJ's Universal Comics, and a woman there named Kat suggested that he reach out to a local

112
00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:21.230
comic book artist named Tone Rodriguez.

113
00:04:21.770 --> 00:04:26.450
Tone had been working on The Simpsons comics for several years, and she thought he might be

114
00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:27.070
a good choice.

115
00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:29.490
We reached out to Tone.

116
00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:29.970
Yes.

117
00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:32.290
And he told us his whole story.

118
00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:34.930
It is amazing, and I cannot wait to share.

119
00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:39.630
So Tone said, one day, Phil just called him out of the blue.

120
00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:40.550
It was a Wednesday.

121
00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.170
And Phil said, I need a comic book drawn up.

122
00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.890
And Tone said, well, when do you need it by?

123
00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:48.410
And Phil said, Monday.

124
00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:51.690
And Tone didn't think he could do it.

125
00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:55.670
He sort of said, I can't turn it around that quickly.

126
00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:01.030
But Phil said, why don't you come down to set so I can better explain what we need?

127
00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:03.370
Phil didn't tell him what show it was.

128
00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:05.150
So Tone didn't know.

129
00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:08.110
He knew he was drawing something for a TV show, but he didn't know what won.

130
00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:09.970
He said he got to the studio.

131
00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:11.830
Nobody was there.

132
00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:13.270
He said, there's no security.

133
00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:14.950
It was a weird part of town.

134
00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:15.390
Yeah.

135
00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:21.130
He said he just walked past the gate, and he started basically just wandering around.

136
00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:22.650
That sounds about right.

137
00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:25.570
Yeah, he was just asking, does anyone know someone named Phil?

138
00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:30.950
Finally, he made it to Phil's prop trailer, where he got introduced to Paul Lieberstein.

139
00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:33.510
They started to explain what we needed.

140
00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:35.130
But then they said, you know what?

141
00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:36.510
This is really Mindy's episode.

142
00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:37.150
She wrote it.

143
00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:38.310
So we should check with her.

144
00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:43.230
Tone said by this point, he realized the show was called The Office, but he'd never seen it.

145
00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:48.470
He said he doesn't watch much TV because if he starts watching something, it really distracts him from his art.

146
00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:52.130
He has to focus, and so he doesn't get into television shows.

147
00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:54.250
I know, right?

148
00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:58.170
So they walk him on set to meet Mindy, and here's what he said.

149
00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:01.030
He said, I'll be 100% honest here.

150
00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:08.150
We were on set waiting for Mindy to give us a heads up on what she wanted for the comic, and when she walked up to the three of us,

151
00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:11.710
I swear I have no recollection of what she had to say.

152
00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:12.330
Nothing.

153
00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:18.110
She walked up to us, and it was like the scene in Wayne's World where Garth sees the woman in the donut shop.

154
00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:22.710
The light radiating from behind her, and you're hearing Dream Weaver by Gary Wright.

155
00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.090
Mindy was stunning.

156
00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:26.390
That's all I can say.

157
00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:30.570
She had me absolutely speechless, and I have no idea what she might have said.

158
00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.050
I had never seen the show.

159
00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.550
Maybe if I had, I would not have been as surprised by her, but I was.

160
00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.670
Again, she was stunning.

161
00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:39.950
Oh my gosh, Mindy.

162
00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:48.130
So he says she explained everything she needed, and at the end of the encounter, she said, did you get that?

163
00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:49.090
And he said, yeah.

164
00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:50.630
And she said, all right.

165
00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:56.210
And she turned and walked away, and at that point, he said, Phil and Paul turned to him and said, did you get that?

166
00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:00.650
And he said, I have no idea what she said.

167
00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:01.610
He was honest?

168
00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:02.170
Yes.

169
00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:06.230
And he said, they must have seen the effect she had on idiots like me before.

170
00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:09.770
Because at this point, they both said, yeah, we know.

171
00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:10.930
We wrote it down for you.

172
00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:11.990
Aw.

173
00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:14.410
And he said, that's a 100% true story.

174
00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:16.570
And then he said, my wife hates that story, huh?

175
00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:24.410
Mindy is someone, when you meet her, she just, she's a captivating person.

176
00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:25.210
Yes.

177
00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:30.510
I mean, she's beautiful, and she has kind of an aura, and she's confident.

178
00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:31.310
Yeah.

179
00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:32.350
I know.

180
00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:33.150
She's a force.

181
00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:34.030
She's a force.

182
00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:37.830
So I asked Tone, how many pages did you illustrate?

183
00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:42.690
And he said that he drew the cover and two interior pages.

184
00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:46.350
He said a full comic book is usually 22 pages.

185
00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:50.290
So that's why he panicked when Phil said they needed it in a few days.

186
00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:52.250
Because he couldn't do 22 pages.

187
00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:52.650
Right, right.

188
00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:56.850
But he said after they explained what Mindy wanted, they said just two pages would be fine,

189
00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:00.530
and they would just duplicate them a couple times to make it look like a full comic book.

190
00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:05.450
Tone said everything in the comic book is hand-drawn, including the logo on the front.

191
00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:10.190
And he said all of the lettering inside was done by his buddy, Tony Fleece.

192
00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:13.290
And he said people just, you didn't do it digitally back then.

193
00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:14.750
Everything was done by hand.

194
00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:15.390
By hand.

195
00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:15.790
Wow.

196
00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:24.710
And he said that the script inside, all the words, he took directly from the note that Phil and Paul had handed him after his meeting with Mindy.

197
00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:25.970
So that's it.

198
00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:27.650
I love that story.

199
00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:34.830
You guys, a few years ago, I was at a comic convention in Philadelphia, and I saw the comic book, The Adventures of Jimmy Halpert.

200
00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.630
I was so excited by it that the vendor gave me two.

201
00:08:37.910 --> 00:08:38.850
I saved them.

202
00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:39.610
I brought them home.

203
00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:40.670
I gave one to Jenna.

204
00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:42.670
So Jenna has one, and I have one.

205
00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:45.250
And I don't know if they're originals.

206
00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:46.570
I'm guessing they're not, right?

207
00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:47.350
Yeah.

208
00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:51.990
Tone said people come up to him all the time and tell him that they purchased one of the original props.

209
00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:57.350
And he said, I hate to disappoint people, but there were only eight of them that were made for the show.

210
00:08:57.710 --> 00:08:59.510
He said he has one.

211
00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:04.550
He gave two to one of his friends in Stockton, California, one to a fan in Bakersfield.

212
00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:14.910
He said there's maybe three original copies out there, but he does make replicas of the book, and he makes them available to different vendors at shows.

213
00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:16.230
Oh, that's fantastic.

214
00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:18.070
We'll have to share that in stories.

215
00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:22.350
Yes, he says there's an email you can use to contact him about getting one.

216
00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:24.390
We can put that in the show notes.

217
00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:29.270
He said they're limited, but you should contact Jacob at that email address.

218
00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:32.570
It's manageinc.art at gmail.com.

219
00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:33.910
But we'll put it in show notes.

220
00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:34.670
We will.

221
00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:37.070
But thank you, Tone, so much.

222
00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:37.830
Yes, Tone.

223
00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:38.250
Thank you.

224
00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:38.750
Thank you.

225
00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:40.610
I always love hearing stories like that.

226
00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:41.190
Me too.

227
00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:47.070
Well, fast fact number two comes to us from Stose B.

228
00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:48.530
in Washington, D.C.

229
00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:55.010
This is a piece of fan mail that just absolutely warmed my heart, and it's episode-specific, and I can't wait to share it.

230
00:09:55.710 --> 00:10:02.070
Stose said, in all capitals, my daughter was born to this episode of The Office.

231
00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:03.110
Oh, my goodness.

232
00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:08.010
I've been waiting to tell this story since she was born on Christmas Eve in 2019.

233
00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:18.590
I went into labor with my first daughter on December 23rd, and as soon as Midnight on Christmas Eve hit, Comedy Central started playing The Office Christmas episodes on repeat.

234
00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:26.810
I kept them on all day, and when the time came to push around 4 p.m., the nurses tried to turn the TV off.

235
00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:32.750
But I kept it on, using the episodes to keep me calm and focused for the next two hours.

236
00:10:32.750 --> 00:10:38.310
And the last thing I remember listening to is Oscar and Ryan's bit about bartending.

237
00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:41.830
It was truly the best way to bring my daughter into the world.

238
00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:45.110
Oh, I love that.

239
00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:46.830
That's hilarious and awesome.

240
00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:49.150
I love that she was like, no, you will not turn that off.

241
00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:49.790
That's right.

242
00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:54.990
And now, fast fact number three, Angela, is all you.

243
00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:58.590
You know it's my favorite thing when you say, can I have a fast fact?

244
00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:02.770
Yes, and it actually pairs very nicely with Stos' story.

245
00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:03.550
Really?

246
00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:04.270
Yes.

247
00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:05.510
Hmm, I'm intrigued.

248
00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:12.030
Well, my fast fact three is all about Ryan and his drink order of an F-Train to Brooklyn extra bitters.

249
00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:12.850
Okay.

250
00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:17.210
